<article class="hiring-page">
  <header class="hiring-header">
    <p class="section-label">Hiring</p>
    <p>
      If you are recruiting me, start here. This is a long-running reference,
      not a status update. It covers where I do my best work, the practical
      shape of a role I can accept, and the interviews I am open to.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="hiring-section hiring-section--reference">
    <div class="hiring-reference-block">
      <h1>Where I do my best work</h1>
      <p class="hiring-lede">
        I am a senior platform engineer, more than ten years in. Platform
        engineer is the clearest-signal title. DevOps is the honest fallback.
        Actual authority and scope matter more than title prestige.
      </p>
      <ul class="hiring-facts">
        <li>
          <strong>Systems integration</strong>
          <span>Across cloud, observability, and developer tooling.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Developer experience</strong>
          <span>
            Internal platforms and developer tooling as the actual product of
            the role. Tooling sold to external customers counts just as
            strongly as tooling built for a team down the hall.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Agent-first infrastructure</strong>
          <span>
            Agentic coding and developer tools, MCP and tool interfaces,
            orchestration, evaluation, observability, safety, and debugging for
            agent consumers. Internal developer platforms where agents are
            first-class users alongside humans belong here too.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Production platforms</strong>
          <span>
            Reliability, incident response, rollout boundaries, and long-term
            operability.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Platform-team interfaces</strong>
          <span>
            Cross-organization work that makes ownership and handoffs explicit.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bounded delivery</strong>
          <span>
            Contract-shaped and embedded missions with real customer problems,
            explicit handoffs, and periodic resets. Modernization work,
            platform control planes, and agentic operations all land here.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Meaningful ownership</strong>
          <span>
            Broad platform territory. Owning wide ground while a company is
            still forming is the strongest version of this, and a
            first-platform-hire charter at an established company is a close
            second.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="hiring-reference-block">
      <h2>Role shape</h2>
      <p>
        Senior is a good fit. Staff or Lead fits when the charter supports real
        authority across a platform or developer-tooling surface. Staff is not
        automatically better than Senior, and a Senior role with real authority
        is not a lesser outcome. What I read is the actual scope, not the title
        tier.
      </p>
      <ul class="hiring-bullets">
        <li>
          A strong platform charter with some SRE or product responsibility can
          fit well.
        </li>
        <li>
          Primarily SRE, operations, or infrastructure maintenance is less
          aligned.
        </li>
        <li>Backend-product work as the actual role is a hard no.</li>
        <li>
          Generic AI branding doesn't add much unless agentic developer
          leverage is part of the work.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="hiring-reference-block">
      <h2>Practical fit</h2>
      <ul class="hiring-facts">
        <li>
          <strong>Location and arrangement</strong>
          <span>
            I am based in the East Bay and I am not relocating. The strongest
            arrangement is a company that is structurally remote, with no
            offices anywhere. A real Bay Area office, or a comfortable Bay Area
            hybrid, works just as well. Hard stop: a company with US offices,
            none of them in the Bay Area. A Bay Area satellite of a company
            headquartered elsewhere is fine, and so is a company whose only
            offices are overseas.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Office cadence</strong>
          <span>
            Up to about three days a week in a Bay Area office works well.
            Four days is workable, but it carries a real cost from the East Bay
            and will slow my answer while I weigh it against other options.
            Five days onsite is a hard stop, however strong the rest of the
            role is. Tell me the expected cadence early, since it is the fact
            most likely to end a conversation that otherwise looked good.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Employment</strong>
          <span>
            Full-time is the default. Direct independent contracting through my
            own business, consultancy work, and embedded delivery can fit. My
            rate is $150 an hour, and I plan at about 70 percent utilisation.
            Staffing-agency W-2 contracts, contract-to-hire, and
            agency-mediated C2C are not a fit.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compensation</strong>
          <span>
            My base-compensation floor depends on the sector: $170K for
            nonprofit and government work, $200K for everyone else. $250K is
            the top of my range. Above the floor, compensation isn't what
            decides it. It's a different unit from the hourly rate above, so
            the two don't convert into each other.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Working environment</strong>
          <span>
            Manager quality, autonomy, team composition, communication style,
            on-call expectations, overtime, and room to work without masking
            all matter. This carries more weight for me than compensation,
            title, or stack. A strong team can make an otherwise ordinary role
            the best one on my list.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Representation and inclusion</strong>
          <span>
            I look at leadership representation, engineering-team composition,
            and the direction a company's public commitments are heading. A
            company walking its commitments back is a mark against it, not a
            neutral fact.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technical environment</strong>
          <span>
            Exact stack matching matters less to me than the shape of the
            problem. My <a href="/resume/">resume</a> is the only source for
            what I actually claim.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Values</strong>
          <span>
            Some product directions and verticals are a flat no for me,
            regardless of how strong the rest of the role is. A technically
            excellent role can still be the wrong role.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="hiring-reference-block">
      <h2>Company stage and stability</h2>
      <p>
        Early and small companies are firmly in scope. Credible traction beats
        late-stage funding, and real users in production tell me more than the
        size of a round.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do look at runway, revenue, and layoff history before I spend a
        hiring loop's worth of time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="hiring-reference-block">
      <h2>Useful context before a first conversation</h2>
      <p>A useful first message includes:</p>
      <ul class="hiring-checklist">
        <li>The name of the company</li>
        <li>Office location and expected cadence</li>
        <li>Base-compensation band</li>
        <li>The actual role charter and ownership boundaries</li>
        <li>Manager and team shape</li>
        <li>On-call and overtime expectations</li>
        <li>Technical environment, product, and customer</li>
        <li>Company stage and interview format</li>
        <li>Why my experience is specifically relevant</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Name the company. A pitch that withholds it is the one most likely to
        expire in my inbox, because I can't evaluate a role I can't look up.
        Direct contact from a founder, hiring manager, or in-house recruiter
        gets a faster and warmer reply from me than agency-mediated outreach
        does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="hiring-section hiring-section--tenure">
    <p class="section-label">On tenure</p>
    <h2>My longest stint is the benchmark, and I'm holding out for it.</h2>
    <p>
      My resume reads short. The number that matters to me is the long one, and
      I'd rather be measured against that than against the average.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm increasingly selective because I want the next chapter to meet or
      exceed that fit, not because I'm trying to talk past the pattern. Ask me
      about any transition and I'll walk you through it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="hiring-section hiring-section--interview">
    <div class="hiring-interview-copy">
      <p class="section-label">Interview format</p>
      <h2>Use the work the role actually requires.</h2>
      <p>
        Senior platform work is systems integration, reliability, cost, and
        organizational interface work. There is plenty of technical depth
        available in formats that test those skills directly.
      </p>

      <h3>Good ways to evaluate me</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>System design</li>
        <li>Architecture deep-dives</li>
        <li>A take-home with a real-codebase prompt</li>
        <li>Paired debugging on actual code</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Hard pass</h3>
      <p>
        I don't do async-proctored puzzle coding. That means a webcam or screen
        share, an automated proctor, a hard time limit, and no human interviewer
        present. This is a hard stop regardless of where the test sits in the
        loop.
      </p>

      <h3>Softer no</h3>
      <p>
        Live puzzle coding with a human interviewer present is a softer no. I
        will sometimes do it when the rest of the loop is strong, but it is not
        where I show my best work.
      </p>
    </div>

